<title>USA: World Bank chief warns of risks of banking crises.</title>
<headline>World Bank chief warns of risks of banking crises.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1996-10-01</dateline>
<text>
<p>About one in five developing countries faces a banking crisis, World Bank President James Wolfensohn said on Tuesday.</p>
<p>Adressing the opening plenary session of the International Monetary Fund/World Bank annual meeting, Wolfensohn pointed to &quot;pervasive problems&quot; with banking regulations and their enforcement.</p>
<p>&quot;That is why the Bank group -- working with the IMF -- is strengthening our capacity to help our clients strengthen their financial sectors,&quot; Wolfensohn said.</p>
<p>Wolfensohn also lashed out against corruption, calling it a problem that all the Bank's 181 member nations need to confront.</p>
<p>&quot;We need to deal with the cancer of corruption,&quot; he said, adding, &quot;The Bank group will not tolerate corruption in the programs that we support.&quot;</p>
</text>
